WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.115
Model shows the architecture behind each showcase.

2
00:00:03.115 --> 00:00:08.250
In this release, it is read-only: there is nothing to drag, remove or

3
00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:08.645
resize.

4
00:00:08.645 --> 00:00:12.595
You can still select Ava's blocks to understand their roles.

5
00:00:12.595 --> 00:00:16.150
Training changes the learned numbers inside this fixed structure.

6
00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:20.904
Follow Ava's diagram from input to output.

7
00:00:20.904 --> 00:00:23.443
Map information becomes numerical representations.

8
00:00:23.443 --> 00:00:29.537
The processing stages transform those representations, then the model scores possible moves.

9
00:00:29.537 --> 00:00:35.122
Blocks marked as architectural extensions add mechanisms beyond the Python baseline.

10
00:00:35.122 --> 00:00:40.200
Their names don't guarantee that a behaviour has been learned.

11
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.825
Chatbot uses the original five-layer language architecture.

12
00:00:44.825 --> 00:00:50.696
Attention combines available information, and a small network refines each token's representation.

13
00:00:50.696 --> 00:00:53.632
The output scores possible next tokens.

14
00:00:53.632 --> 00:00:56.568
Repeating that prediction produces an answer.

15
00:00:56.568 --> 00:01:01.950
Device messaging uses the same architecture with its own saved learning.

16
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:07.022
The language architecture has just over one million trainable weights.

17
00:01:07.022 --> 00:01:12.056
Training adjusts those weights using your examples; it doesn't add words or layers.

18
00:01:12.056 --> 00:01:15.928
In Playground, those weights stay fixed while the model predicts.

19
00:01:15.928 --> 00:01:19.800
That distinction lets you explore learning without rebuilding the machinery.
